From b7135f06bb1988bdd37354f63ed918133e101342 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 09:40:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] syspipe: c is volatile (cclone, walk and open change it after
 waserror, its handler closes it)

syspipe sets c[1] = cclone(c[0]) after its waserror(), walks both and
replaces both with what the device's open returns; an error on the way
(Egreg, the open, Enofd) reaches the handler that closes c[0] and c[1].
Without volatile they are indeterminate there (C11 7.13.2.1, as 0066):
a compiler that does not keep the array in memory may close c[1] as nil
(the clone leaks) or a chan the walk replaced.  Lower risk than the
others (the array's address goes to walk and newfd2), but the same
rule.  newfd2 takes a Chan *volatile c[2].  Found by a review of the
namec volatile patch (2026-11-27); compiled with gcc and clang, not run.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c b/sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c
index 72faac684..cbbaeb723 100644
--- a/sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c
+++ b/sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c
@@ -110,7 +110,7 @@ newfd(Chan *c, int mode)
 }
 
 static int
-newfd2(int fd[2], Chan *c[2])
+newfd2(int fd[2], Chan *volatile c[2])
 {
 	Fgrp *f;
 
@@ -210,7 +210,7 @@ syspipe(va_list list)
 {
 	static char *datastr[] = {"data", "data1"};
 	int fd[2], *ufd;
-	Chan *c[2];
+	Chan *volatile c[2];	/* cclone, walk and open change them after waserror, its handler closes them */
 
 	ufd = va_arg(list, int*);
 	validaddr((uintptr)ufd, sizeof(fd), 1);
-- 
